.\" robodr-enroll(8). Source of truth: the USAGE text in
.\" crates/robodr-fleet/src/bin/robodr-enroll.rs, the crate docs and
.\" docs/contracts/robodr.fleet-api.md. Keep them in sync.
.TH ROBODR\-ENROLL 8 "2026-10-26" "RoboDR 0.1.0-alpha.1" "RoboDR Manual"
.SH NAME
robodr\-enroll \- enroll a RoboDR unit with the SOC, rotate its keys, take revocation lists, profiles and ring information from the SOC
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B robodr\-enroll
.RB [ enroll ]
.RB ( \-\-token\-file
.I FILE
|
.BR "\-\-token \-" )
.RB [ \-\-reuse\-existing\-keys ]
.br
.B robodr\-enroll request
.RB ( \-\-token\-file
.I FILE
|
.BR "\-\-token \-" )
.B \-\-out
.I FILE
.br
.B robodr\-enroll accept \-\-answer
.I FILE
.br
.B robodr\-enroll rotate \-\-role
.BR uplink | finding
.RB [ \-\-finish ]
.RB [ \-\-request\-out
.I FILE
|
.B \-\-answer
.IR FILE ]
.br
.B robodr\-enroll rotate \-\-role
.BR uplink | finding
.B \-\-cancel
.RB [ \-\-request\-out
.I FILE
|
.B \-\-answer
.IR FILE ]
.br
.B robodr\-enroll rotate \-\-role
.BR uplink | finding
.B \-\-abort
.br
.B robodr\-enroll revocations
.RB [ fetch ]
.RB [ \-\-platform
.IR P ]
.br
.B robodr\-enroll revocations install \-\-file
.I FILE
.RB [ \-\-platform
.IR P ]
.br
.B robodr\-enroll profile publish \-\-draft
.I FILE
.br
.B robodr\-enroll profile fetch
.br
.B robodr\-enroll profile stage \-\-profile
.I FILE
.RB [ \-\-sig
.IR FILE ]
.br
.B robodr\-enroll profile approve
.RB ( \-\-approval
.I FILE
|
.BR \-\-fetch )
.br
.B robodr\-enroll profile status
.br
.B robodr\-enroll ring
.RB [ \-\-file
.IR FILE ]
.br
.B robodr\-enroll status
.SH DESCRIPTION
The robot side of the fleet lifecycle (contract
.IR /usr/share/doc/robodr/contracts/robodr.fleet\-api.md ).
Run as root on the unit (or its companion computer); it reads and writes
.IR /etc/robodr/keys ,
agentd's profile, trust and revocation files, and its own state directory
.IR /var/lib/robodr/fleet .
Two instances never run on one state directory (an
.BR flock (2)
on
.IR fleet.lock ).
Nothing the SOC sends is executed: tokens, answers, approvals and ring
information are verified data; a profile takes effect only through agentd's
own verification at its next start.
.SS Enrollment
.B enroll
(the default command) verifies the single-use enrollment token
.RI ( rdet1. ...,
signed by a SOC fleet key configured in
.IR fleet.json ,
never taken from the token), checks its validity on the unit's clock and its
workspace, robot and site against the unit's configuration, generates the
unit's
.BR uplink ,
.B finding
and
.B blackbox
Ed25519 keys on the unit (mode 0600, the public halves as
.IR .pub ),
sends the enrollment request (public keys, agentd version, profile digest;
signed by each of the three new keys, proving possession of each, with an
HMAC under the token secret; the token itself is never sent) and verifies
the SOC-signed answer.
.PP
The token is a secret until the SOC consumes it and is never accepted on
the command line (it would show in
.IR /proc/<pid>/cmdline ,
sudo logs and the shell history):
.B \-\-token\-file
names a file that must be owned by root and not accessible by group or
others (mode 0600; destroy it afterwards with
.BR "shred \-u" ),
.B "\-\-token \-"
reads it from standard input. An existing key
file is refused unless
.B \-\-reuse\-existing\-keys
(e.g. the black-box key of an agentd that already runs). A token is used
once; a request whose answer was lost is resent unchanged by running the
same command again.
.PP
Air-gapped units:
.B request \-\-out
writes the request to a file (no secret in it) for the SOC on vetted media;
.B accept \-\-answer
verifies the SOC's answer file and finishes.
.SS Key rotation
.B rotate \-\-role uplink|finding
generates the new key next to the active one
.RI ( <key>.next ),
sends the rotate request signed by the old and the new key, and, once the
SOC confirms (both keys valid: overlap), swaps the files atomically
.RB ( rename (2)),
keeps the old key in
.I <keydir>/retired/
and records a key handover signed by both keys in the fleet journal. Then
restart the service that uses the key
.RB ( robodr\-uplink
or
.BR robodr\-agentd ),
and when it runs with the new key (for
.BR finding :
and the findings signed with the old key are delivered) run
.B rotate \-\-role R \-\-finish
to retire the old key at the SOC; only then is the old key shredded. Every
step is journaled; after a crash the same command resumes.
.B \-\-request\-out
and
.B \-\-answer
do the same with files.
.PP
Once the rotate request has been sent (or written with
.BR \-\-request\-out ),
the SOC may hold the rotation even when its answer was lost, so the
rotation is never abandoned locally:
.B \-\-abort
only discards a rotation whose request never left the unit. Otherwise run
the same command again (the SOC answers with its stored answer), or
.B rotate \-\-role R \-\-cancel
to end it at the SOC: the cancel request is signed by the old key, the SOC
retires the new key (or records the rotation id as cancelled if it never
saw it), and the unit shreds the new key; the old key stays active. The
black-box key is not rotated by this tool (OPERATIONS 4.3).
.SS Revocation lists, profiles, ring
.B revocations
fetches the root-signed revocation list for the unit's platform, verifies it
against agentd's trust roots, refuses an older serial or the same serial
with other content, installs it at agentd's
.I revocation_list
path and warns when agentd would refuse the active profile with it.
.PP
.B profile publish
sends a draft profile for review.
.B profile fetch
(or
.B profile stage
with files) verifies the SOC's signed profile with agentd's own trust
inputs and anti-rollback state and
.B stages
it: it is not enforced.
.B profile approve
installs the staged profile at agentd's
.I profile_path
only with an approval artefact signed by a SOC
.B approval key
.RI ( soc.approval_keys ,
never the online fleet key) for exactly this robot and profile digest,
valid at most 7 days, unexpired and not used before; restart agentd to
enforce it. The new profile and
.I .sig
are both written completely before the two renames
.RI ( .sig
first); a crash between them leaves a pair agentd refuses (observe-only)
until
.B approve
is run again.
.PP
.B ring
fetches the SOC's update-ring information (ring 0/1/2, target release,
minimum release), verifies it (a document issued in the future is refused)
and writes
.I /var/lib/robodr/fleet/ring.env
for
.BR install\-bundle.sh ,
which raises the unit's security floor to the minimum release. Otherwise
information only: nothing is downloaded or installed.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-config " FILE"
fleet configuration (default
.IR /etc/robodr/fleet.json ;
missing default file: built-in defaults).
.TP
.BI \-\-soc\-url " URL"
the SOC API root,
.IR https://host/api .
.TP
.BI \-\-ca\-bundle " PEM"
the SOC's CA certificate(s), the only TLS trust anchor.
.TP
.BI \-\-spki\-sha256 " HEX"
pin the SOC server key (repeatable).
CRLs of the SOC's CAs are configured in
.I fleet.json
.BR soc.crl_files :
a revoked SOC certificate is refused, and so is every SOC server while a
CRL is missing for its CA, expired or unusable.
.TP
.BI \-\-token\-file " FILE"
the enrollment token, in a file owned by root, mode 0600.
.TP
.B "\-\-token \-"
read the enrollment token from standard input (any other value of
.B \-\-token
is refused).
.TP
.BI \-\-soc\-key " FILE"
SOC fleet public key file (repeatable): owned by root, not writable by
group or others, in a directory not writable by group or others.
.TP
.BI \-\-approval\-key " FILE"
SOC approval public key file (repeatable; the same rules): the only keys a
profile approval is accepted from; never one of the fleet keys.
.TP
.BI \-\-agentd\-config " FILE"
agentd's configuration (robot id, site, profile path and trust inputs).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/robodr/fleet.json
configuration (example:
.IR /usr/share/doc/robodr/examples/fleet.example.json ).
.TP
.IR /etc/robodr/fleet\-soc.pub ", " /etc/robodr/fleet\-approval.pub
the SOC's fleet key and approval key (from the SOC on vetted media).
.TP
.I /var/lib/robodr/fleet/
state: enrollment, rotation journals, staged profile, replay guards, the
fleet journal
.RI ( journal/ ,
a robodr-blackbox recorder signed with the black-box key).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 success; 1 refused (verification, binding, rollback, SOC refusal); 2
usage error; 3 the SOC was not reachable (retry: requests are resent
unchanged).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR robodr\-agentd (8),
.BR robodr\-commission (1),
.BR robodr\-uplink (1),
.BR robodr\-blackbox (1);
.IR /usr/share/doc/robodr/OPERATIONS.md ,
section 8.6;
.IR /usr/share/doc/robodr/contracts/robodr.fleet\-api.md .
